1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,741
Maya invested $48,000.

2
00:00:02,791 --> 00:00:05,540
Jake invested almost four times that.

3
00:00:05,590 --> 00:00:07,790
Same salary. Same investment.

4
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:13,038
At 65, Maya is richer. And Jake never catches up.

5
00:00:13,988 --> 00:00:16,737
This is Maya. This is Jake.

6
00:00:16,787 --> 00:00:21,577
They graduate the same year, start the same kind of job,

7
00:00:21,627 --> 00:00:25,192
and both earn $60,000 a year.

8
00:00:25,242 --> 00:00:31,664
They're fictional, but every number in this video is real math you can check yourself.

9
00:00:31,714 --> 00:00:35,280
At 22, Maya makes one decision.

10
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:42,160
She takes $400 from every month's pay, about 8% of her salary,

11
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:47,408
and puts it into a simple index fund. Then she ignores it.

12
00:00:47,458 --> 00:00:52,248
Jake? Jake has other plans. A new car. A nicer apartment.

13
00:00:52,298 --> 00:00:57,904
"I'll start investing when I'm older," he says. "When I actually make money."

14
00:00:57,954 --> 00:01:03,968
But here's the twist: Maya is only going to do this for ten years.

15
00:01:04,918 --> 00:01:09,708
Maya invests $400 a month for exactly ten years.

16
00:01:09,758 --> 00:01:18,221
By 32, she's put in $48,000, and her account has grown to about $69,000.

17
00:01:18,271 --> 00:01:21,020
Then life happens. Kids. A mortgage.

18
00:01:21,070 --> 00:01:25,044
She stops investing. Completely. Not one more dollar, ever.

19
00:01:25,094 --> 00:01:29,067
And at that exact moment, Jake finally wakes up.

20
00:01:29,117 --> 00:01:36,764
He's 32, he's earning more, and he starts investing the same $400 a month.

21
00:01:36,814 --> 00:01:45,686
But Jake doesn't stop. He invests every single month for 33 years, all the way to 65.

22
00:01:45,736 --> 00:01:51,750
In total, he puts in $190,000. Almost four times what Maya invested.

23
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:54,957
So who ends up with more money?

24
00:01:55,907 --> 00:01:58,107
Let's fast-forward.

25
00:01:58,157 --> 00:02:07,029
At 45, Jake has been investing for 13 years. He has about $101,000. Not bad.

26
00:02:07,079 --> 00:02:14,726
But Maya, who hasn't added a single dollar in 13 years, has about $172,000.

27
00:02:14,776 --> 00:02:20,790
At 55, Jake has about $273,000. Maya: $345,000.

28
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:23,997
And at 65, retirement day:

29
00:02:24,047 --> 00:02:29,245
Jake has about $618,000. Maya has about $693,000.

30
00:02:29,295 --> 00:02:35,309
Jake invested $142,000 more than Maya, and still ended up with less.

31
00:02:35,359 --> 00:02:43,006
So how is this possible? It comes down to one thing that doesn't care how hard you work.

32
00:02:43,956 --> 00:02:46,156
It's called compound growth.

33
00:02:46,206 --> 00:02:50,588
Your money earns returns, and then those returns earn returns.

34
00:02:50,638 --> 00:02:54,203
Think of it like a snowball rolling downhill.

35
00:02:54,253 --> 00:03:00,676
In year one, Maya's snowball is tiny. But every year it picks up more snow,

36
00:03:00,726 --> 00:03:04,699
and the bigger it gets, the faster it grows.

37
00:03:04,749 --> 00:03:10,355
At 7% a year, money roughly doubles about every ten years.

38
00:03:10,405 --> 00:03:16,828
Maya's first dollars had 43 years to grow. That's more than four doublings.

39
00:03:16,878 --> 00:03:24,933
A single dollar she invested at 22 turned into about $20 by 65.

40
00:03:24,983 --> 00:03:30,997
Jake's first dollars only had 33 years. About $10 each.

41
00:03:31,047 --> 00:03:38,286
And the dollars he invested at 60? They barely had time to grow at all.

42
00:03:38,336 --> 00:03:45,166
Maya didn't win because she saved more. She won because she gave her money more time.

43
00:03:45,216 --> 00:03:50,006
In investing, time is the one ingredient you can't buy back.

44
00:03:50,056 --> 00:03:58,111
One quick note: we're assuming 7% a year, roughly the US stock market's long-run average after inflation.

45
00:03:58,161 --> 00:04:03,767
Some years are much better, some are much worse, and nothing is guaranteed.

46
00:04:03,817 --> 00:04:08,607
But over decades, the pattern stays the same: earlier beats bigger.

47
00:04:09,557 --> 00:04:12,714
But wait. What if Maya never stopped?

48
00:04:12,764 --> 00:04:20,003
Same $400 a month, from 22 all the way to 65.

49
00:04:20,053 --> 00:04:23,618
She'd retire with about $1.3 million.

50
00:04:23,668 --> 00:04:32,132
For Jake to catch that, starting at 32, he'd need to invest about $850 a month.

51
00:04:32,182 --> 00:04:36,971
More than double. That's the real cost of waiting ten years.

52
00:04:37,921 --> 00:04:42,303
Now, if you're thinking, "Great, I'm already Jake," don't worry.

53
00:04:42,353 --> 00:04:47,959
Jake still retired with over $600,000. Starting late beats never starting.

54
00:04:48,009 --> 00:04:52,391
Here's how to be more like Maya, starting this month.

55
00:04:52,441 --> 00:04:58,455
One: if your job offers a 401(k) match, take all of it.

56
00:04:58,505 --> 00:05:04,111
A match is part of your pay you only get if you contribute.

57
00:05:04,161 --> 00:05:10,176
Two: start with any amount. Even $50 a month builds the habit.

58
00:05:10,226 --> 00:05:15,423
Three: automate it, so it comes out before you see your paycheck.

59
00:05:15,473 --> 00:05:22,304
Four: keep it simple. A low-cost, broad index fund owns a slice of the whole market.

60
00:05:22,354 --> 00:05:27,960
Five: don't panic when the market drops. Maya's money went through crashes too.

61
00:05:28,010 --> 00:05:30,210
She just didn't sell.

62
00:05:31,160 --> 00:05:34,317
Maya and Jake earned the same salary.

63
00:05:34,367 --> 00:05:40,382
They weren't different in talent or luck. The difference was one decision about time.

64
00:05:40,432 --> 00:05:46,446
The best day to start was ten years ago. The second-best day is today.

65
00:05:46,496 --> 00:05:54,551
Subscribe so you don't miss the next one: how to budget a $2,500 paycheck, step by step.
